A||||14||GT Bastard||Philisophical implications of the George Foreman Grill||||5||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||05-23-2000||01:39 PM||||A couple backyard mechanics were mentioning that if you rebuild just the top end of a motor, you'll risk putting too much pressure on the weaker bottom end.  They advised me to forget about rebuilding the heads and just have them ported.  Does this sound like BS?  I was a little bit skeptical, but I think what they are saying makes sense.  So what's the scoop?  BTW, my heads should be ported by the end of the day!  whhooo hooo||||reg||5
Z||000001||mbramble||05-23-2000||01:55 PM||||Can't answer your question but your post about replacing/repairing one part causing a weaker part to go reminded me of a problem with brake lines.  It was about a hundred years ago (it seems so anyway).  I was a young Seaman stationed up in Winter Harbor, ME.  Had an old rusted car that a brake line went out on.  Having little money or car knowledge, I went and bought a replacement for the rusted section.  Installed it, bled the system and it worked great  -- for about two stops.  Then, another section went out.  Replaced it, another section.  Finally wised up and replaced them all!!  Another, very similar situation happened on my old Ford LTD that I took to Guam when I got stationed there.  Had the front calipers rebuilt at a local brake shop.  First time the guy pressed the brakes when he was testing them,  the brake lines ruptured in 2 or 3 places.  Had to call my Dad in the States to have him mail me some replacements  (instead of letting the brake shop do it and charging me 5x what my Dad got them for).<P>Sorry for the long post.  Ya just never know what will set off old memories!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||05-23-2000||02:35 PM||||um. the george foreman grill is cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||05-23-2000||02:53 PM||||i enjoy my Lean Mean Grilling Machine (tm)!  it does wonders on boneless porkchops and chicken brest!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||05-23-2000||03:28 PM||||GT, I had the same experience as mbramble with my Fiero cooling system.  First, found a leaky hose at the thermostat housing.  Fixed that, and the radiator blew.  Replaced that and the heater core went south.  Replaced that and another hoes burst.  It seemed that the weakest link would break.  The coolant and brake systems are like this because you are talking about a pressurised system.  <P>The engine on the otherhand may be a different kind of beast.  If you have good compression now, then rebuilding the top end won't put any more pressure on the pistons, rods, crank, and bearings than it already has.  So why would something break that wasn't about to anyway.<P>If you are going to have the heads off for porting, you might as well have a valve job done.  Personally, if I am going in deep enough to take the heads off, I would likely re-ring at the very least and probably put new crank and rod bearings in as well.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||05-23-2000||04:50 PM||||The George Foreman Grill is the most important kitchen appliance that a bachelor can own.  It will revolutionize the way one eats.<P>It is essential for the Y2K Kitchen.  <P>Those of you who have not yet seen the way of the lean mean grilling machine, Fear Not. There is still time for enlightenment.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||05-23-2000||04:57 PM||||I just talked to my dad. Apparently the jackass who rebuilt the motor before only put in new intake valves.  He said the intake valves were like new, but the exhaust valves were pretty lousy.  So it looks like it's getting some new valves and some other misc. machine work.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||05-23-2000||05:14 PM||||GTB,<P>I've heard that before and personally put it in the BS catagory. If the bottom end gives out after reworking a head that just means you should have rebuilt the whole thing to start with... NOT that you caused it by doing the top end.<P>just my .02...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Zman302||05-24-2000||01:05 AM||||What they told you was true.  The bottom end should always be built first then worry about the top end.  The increase of power from your mods to the top end will find the weak points of the bottom and end up trashing everything.  Rods thru the block and broken pistons tend to ruin more than just the bottom end.  just my 2 cents.<P>                    Craig<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Zman302||05-24-2000||01:05 AM||||What they told you was true.  The bottom end should always be built first then worry about the top end.  The increase of power from your mods to the top end will find the weak points of the bottom and end up trashing everything.  Rods thru the block and broken pistons tend to ruin more than just the bottom end.  just my 2 cents.<P>                    Craig<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Zman302||05-24-2000||01:05 AM||||What they told you was true.  The bottom end should always be built first then worry about the top end.  The increase of power from your mods to the top end will find the weak points of the bottom and end up trashing everything.  Rods thru the block and broken pistons tend to ruin more than just the bottom end.  just my 2 cents.<P>                    Craig<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||05-24-2000||10:02 AM||||The grill is nice as long as you don't buy into the reduced fat thing. Consumer reports found it wasn't any better that anything else in that department. Problem with regular frying is most people leave food in the pan soaking up grease after cooking. The forman grill does prevent that. being 2 sided i expect it works pretty quick. (Same principle as a waffle iron.)<P>As for the motor thing.......<P>It depends entirly on the condition of the motor as a whole. If you pull off the head and see a bunch of bad cylinder wear it's likely rebuld time all around. I'd also check the crank and rods for obvious damage or play.<P>I've had just heads rebuilt. As long as the bottom doesn't already have a problem the head won't cause more trouble. I burnt a valve on one ford 2 litre rebuilt the head, and drove it for 40-50 thousand miles more before the bottom finally wore out. When I did it I also checked out the bottom to make sure it wasn't getting ready to cough up a bearing or something.<P>It also depends on what you are going to do with it latter. if you're going to beat on it just rebuild the whole thing now. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||05-24-2000||10:23 AM||||The wife wanted the family size for Christmas so we put it in layaway and promptly forgot about it.  We went back and got our $5 deposit back a few weeks ago.  Now the store's out.  Guess we'll just have to wait for the next shipment not that it really matters.  We're never home long enough to cook anything anyway.  Wait.  My wife cooked me some minute rice last night.  She said it took 5 minutes.  What's up with that??<P>About the motor...Beats me.||||reg||1
Z||000013||GT Bastard||05-24-2000||02:52 PM||||Monkey, I noticed the same thing about minute rice.... The first time I cooked it I was p*ssed off.  The should be sued for false advertising.  It should be called "Several Minute Rice."<P>About the motor... The bottom end shows hardly any wear.  The cylinder walls are smooth as heck.  There is a very slight ridge at the top, but part of that just looks like carbon build-up.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||DJRice||05-25-2000||11:10 AM||||I can take a Bubba Burger right out of the freezer and toss it on the Lean Mean Grilling Machine and it will be nicely cooked in about 5 minutes.  Same thing with a frozen chicken breast.  A steak takes about 7 minutes to get it how I like it.<P>I dont buy in to the fat reducing thing either, but it is convenient.||||reg||1
